From b9ffa957a355166d6846ad1789e26c6171df47f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shk <shkaaang@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 16:18:27 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] feat(oliveyoung): review_low reads one board's low-rated end
 fully

The `review` walk samples: two products a board, five boards, two sorts, three
pages. An analysis needs the whole low-rated end of a few products instead --
RATING_ASC is every one-star, then every two-star, recency as the tiebreak
within a rating, so read to the end it is the complete low end, and a daily
re-walk picks up new complaints on a site with no newest-first sort.

`run --dataset review_low`: the suncare board's top 10, each up to six
RATING_ASC pages of 50 (the cursor walk stops early on hasNext=false), one
RATING_DESC page as a satisfaction baseline, and the stats request. 81 of the
budget's 100. Same record types and tables as `review`; scope records it under
its own name so the two walks' row counts cannot be confused.

The page cap is now carried on the request (`pages` in Fetch.context) instead
of read from REVIEW_PAGES in `_next_page`: both walks share the endpoint and
parser and differ in exactly that number. Requests without it default to
REVIEW_PAGES, so the `review` walk is unchanged. max_depth is the deeper of
the two walks rather than REVIEW_PAGES.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
